% ============================================================================
% abo-genotype-phenotype — for each of three named ABO genotype combinations,
% the blood-type PHENOTYPE it produces, a fresh WebFetch-sourced OpenStax
% table (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY). Grounds the "heredity" Major Gap
% ADJ-STDLIB-COVERAGE.md §5.1/§5.2 names for K-8/9-12 science — the THIRD
% instance of that gap (after `biology/dna-base-pairs.adj` and
% `biology/heredity-term.adj`), and the first to ground it with a REAL,
% textbook-solid genetics example rather than either a molecular fact
% (base-pairing) or curated abstract vocabulary.
% ============================================================================
% `biology/heredity-term.adj` already defines the abstract vocabulary this
% table puts to work — "dominant" ("one allele is expressed and the effect
% of the other allele is masked"), "genotype" ("the type of variant present
% at a given location"), "phenotype" ("an individual's observable traits") —
% but that table's own header explicitly documents checking twice for a
% composable second table and finding the search dry: no other shipped table
% has `gene`, `allele`, `dominant`, `recessive`, `genotype`, or `phenotype`
% as a ROW VALUE anywhere in the stdlib (confirmed again this session via a
% fresh full-tree grep). So this file does not compose `heredity-term.adj`
% directly — it grounds a genuinely NEW, concrete worked example of the same
% underlying concepts `heredity-term.adj` only defines in the abstract, one
% that a K-12 genetics curriculum reaches for constantly: human ABO blood
% type is the textbook case of MULTIPLE ALLELES and CODOMINANCE, not the
% simple one-dominant-one-recessive picture MS-LS3 vocabulary starts from.
%
% CANDIDATE SOURCES TRIED, in order (repeating `heredity-term.adj`'s own
% "vet before shipping" discipline, since this domain has a documented
% myth-trap precedent):
%   1. The NCBI Bookshelf "The ABO blood group" chapter (already cited by
%      `biology/blood-groups.adj`) states the GENERAL facts plainly — three
%      alleles (A, B, O), "the A and B antigens were inherited codominantly
%      over O", and "six possible genotypes and four possible blood types
%      (phenotypes)" — but never enumerates, in continuous prose, WHICH of
%      the six genotypes maps to which of the four phenotypes; that mapping
%      appears only in a non-text figure (Figure 8.14-style diagrams appear
%      in secondary sources reproducing this same chapter). Byte-for-byte
%      curl-verified: no substring "AA", "AO", "BO", or "OO" (as genotype
%      labels) appears anywhere in the chapter's text. REJECTED as the
%      source for the genotype->phenotype mapping specifically (though it
%      remains `blood-groups.adj`'s own correct citation for the antigen
%      table).
%   2. OpenStax "Concepts of Biology", section 8.3 "Extensions of the Laws
%      of Inheritance" — WebFetched and curl-verified byte-for-byte against
%      the raw server-rendered page text (not an AI paraphrase). Its
%      Codominance subsection states, in ONE continuous passage, EXACTLY
%      three of the six possible genotype->phenotype pairings in plain
%      prose — the two homozygotes and the one heterozygote a self-cross
%      between them produces — which is what the table below grounds. The
%      other three genotypes (a heterozygote crossed with the null allele,
%      and the null/null homozygote) are true too, but this specific passage
%      never states them in continuous prose (only in an accompanying
%      figure this fetch could not extract as text), so this table honestly
%      STOPS at the three it can cite, rather than completing the set from
%      memory. OpenStax is CC-BY-licensed, peer-reviewed, and openly
%      accessible — a `trust consensus` source, the same tier this stdlib
%      already gives CK-12 (`scientific-model-type.adj`) and Mathematics
%      LibreTexts (`geometry/angle-pairs.adj`), one rung below a primary
%      government reference.
%
% A full-tree grep for `\bcodominan`, `\bmultiple allele`, and the literal
% row atoms below, run before scoping, found zero prior hits — this is the
% FIRST genuine ABO-genetics (as opposed to ABO-immunohematology) content
% anywhere in this stdlib.
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? abo_genotype_phenotype(ia_ia, $BloodType)
%       % a
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `abo_genotype_phenotype(genotype, blood_type)` carrying the
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the blood type WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model
% calls, and abstains on any genotype this curated slice does not include
% (see below). The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a blood type and recall
% which genotype the source's own passage says produces it:
%
%     ? abo_genotype_phenotype($G, ab)
%       % ia_ib
%
% The three genotype/phenotype pairs this passage states, as a truth table:
%
%     genotype   the source's OWN notation   blood_type (phenotype)   atom
%     ---------  --------------------------  -----------------------  -----
%     ia_ia      "Homozygotes (IAIA ...)"     A                        a
%     ib_ib      "... and IBIB) express ..."  B                        b
%     ia_ib      "heterozygotes (IAIB) ..."   AB                       ab
%
% (`IA`/`IB` render as capital I with a superscript A or B in the source's
% own typography — flattened here to plain adjacent letters, matching the
% source's own HTML with no inserted spacing between the two allele symbols
% of a genotype.)
%
% WHY this is a genuine "heredity" fact, not a coincidental relabeling of
% anything already shipped: `biology/blood-groups.adj` tables WHICH antigen
% a given PHENOTYPE (a/b/ab/o) carries — a pure immunohematology fact with no
% genetics content at all (its own header never mentions an allele, a gene,
% or a genotype). This table answers a different, upstream question: WHICH
% phenotype does a given GENOTYPE (a specific pair of inherited alleles)
% produce — the actual heredity mechanism `heredity-term.adj`'s "genotype"
% and "phenotype" entries define only in the abstract. Because this table's
% `blood_type` column deliberately reuses the SAME atoms (`a`, `b`, `ab`)
% `blood-groups.adj`'s `blood_type` column already uses, a later rule could
% chain the two — genotype -> phenotype -> antigen — into a single derived
% fact a clinical or genetics reasoner could use directly; this file does
% not ship that rule (kept as a separate, smaller slice per this loop's
% "keep each slice small and citable" discipline), but the shared vocabulary
% is deliberate, not accidental.
%
% HONEST ABSTENTION — the crux of why this table is narrow rather than
% overreaching: the cited passage states only three of the six genotypes
% NCBI's chapter confirms exist (`ia_i`, `ib_i`, and `i_i` — a heterozygote
% with the null allele, and the null/null homozygote — are real, textbook
% genotypes, and their phenotypes follow logically from the same passage's
% own dominance statement elsewhere in the chapter, "the IA and IB alleles
% are codominant with each other and are both dominant over the i allele" —
% but that sentence never itself states a phenotype LABEL the way the
% Codominance passage's three pairs do, so deriving `ia_i -> a` would mean
% inferring rather than reading a stated fact). A query for any of these
% three, or for a typo, honestly ABSTAINS rather than inventing a blood
% type — the engine never completes the set from outside knowledge.
%
% Provenance: WebFetch-verified directly against the raw OpenStax
% server-rendered page text before writing this file, confirming the quoted
% span byte-for-byte, including its exact three-genotype scope (no fourth,
% fifth, or sixth genotype mentioned in continuous prose anywhere on the
% page). `locator` is the live OpenStax URL. `trust consensus` — OpenStax
% ("Concepts of Biology", Rice University, CC-BY 4.0) is a peer-reviewed,
% openly licensed educational reference, not a primary government source,
% hence one rung below `heredity-term.adj`'s and `blood-groups.adj`'s own
% `authoritative` NHGRI/NCBI tier. Nothing here is asserted from memory.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table abo_genotype_phenotype {
    columns genotype, blood_type

    row (ia_ia, a)
    row (ib_ib, b)
    row (ia_ib, ab)

    source "Homozygotes (IAIA and IBIB) express either the A or the B phenotype, and heterozygotes (IAIB) express both phenotypes equally. The IAIB individual has blood type AB."
    locator "https://openstax.org/books/concepts-biology/pages/8-3-extensions-of-the-laws-of-inheritance"
    trust consensus
}
